<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Champagne</h1><p class="st-meta">950 CE – 1314 CE (approx.) · capital Troyes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The county of the great Champagne fairs, where Flemish cloth met Italian merchants and the court of Troyes nurtured Chrétien de Troyes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a> were built up in the 10th century by the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-vermandois">Vermandois</a>, descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-ii-count-of-vermandois">Herbert II</a>, and passed around 1022 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-ii-of-blois">Odo II of Blois</a>. A younger branch of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-blois">Blois</a> family took Troyes, and Count Hugh was styled count of Champagne by around 1102. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-ii-count-of-champagne">Theobald II</a> reunited Blois and Champagne in the 12th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts protected the cycle of fairs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/provins">Provins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagny-sur-marne">Lagny</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bar-sur-aube">Bar-sur-Aube</a>, which made Champagne the commercial crossroads of 12th- and 13th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-i-count-of-champagne">Henry the Liberal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-countess-of-champagne">Marie de Champagne</a> made Troyes a literary centre, home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-i-of-navarre">Theobald IV</a>, a trouvère poet, became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> in 1234.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-i-of-navarre">Joan I</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV</a> in 1284, and when their son Louis X became king in 1314, Champagne passed into the royal domain of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>. The fairs had already declined as sea routes to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a> opened.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-champagne.jpg" alt="County of Champagne" width="320" height="313"><figcaption>Image: ZigeunerAlt, revised crop Ulamm · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_France_en_1180_(section).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 CE – 1314 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Troyes</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry I of Champagne, Theobald IV of Champagne, Joan I of Navarre</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Champagne, County of Troyes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1132&amp;focus=county-of-champagne" aria-label="County of Champagne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4775 488 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Champagne lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5095 744L5054 728M5095 744L4960 913"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M4960 913h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5095 744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5095 744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Troyes. <a href="/atlas?year=1132&amp;focus=county-of-champagne">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
